#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304} :activity/id #uuid "00d7af21-acdd-52ae-8ffc-37290b0f0c74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125741 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$*\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental link between calculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)= x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$ It is easy to see that $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$*\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, $$\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.$$\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$ is parallel to the level curve.\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain $$\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35b375f8-a01b-56db-97b7-ac88f2a7883a" :step/math-academy-id 11200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124467 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050994}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385514}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "74d5fcb5-d32b-5ddd-92cd-491ff00e4b54" :step/math-academy-id 11376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124468 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124272 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385528}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "147b9868-60b3-5d25-8663-966137fb6540" :step/math-academy-id 11379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125746 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if the gradient vector is nonzero, i.e.,\n\n$$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$w = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$\n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt] &= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt] &= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt] \\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle. }$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2524 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "81eef922-1c17-502c-89a2-3e132f4a64e6" :step/math-academy-id 11383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124273 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "20b0d4d3-7059-5c01-9a72-d14629d41a1c" :step/math-academy-id 11384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124274 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406521}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "266600d8-59f0-586e-ba73-083efb5b7259" :step/math-academy-id 11387}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124737}]}
